46.102 Policy

L0:Agencies must ensure that-
L1:(a) Contracts include inspection and other quality requirements, including warranty clauses when appropriate, that are determined necessary to protect the Government’s interest;
L1:(b) Supplies or services (including commercial services) tendered by contractors meet contract requirements;
L1:(c) Government contract quality assurance is conducted before acceptance (except as otherwise provided in this part), by or under the direction of Government personnel;
L1:(d) No contract precludes the Government from performing inspection; and
L1:(e) Nonconforming supplies or services are rejected, except as otherwise provided in 46.407.

46.103 Contracting office responsibilities

L0:Contracting offices are responsible for-
L1:(a) Receiving from the activity responsible for technical requirements any specifications for inspection, testing, and other contract quality requirements essential to ensure the integrity of the supplies or services (the activity responsible for technical requirements is responsible for prescribing contract quality requirements, such as inspection and testing requirements or, for service contracts, a quality assurance surveillance plan);
L1:(b) Including in solicitations and contracts the appropriate requirements for the contractor’s control of quality for the supplies or services to be acquired;
L1:(c) Issuing any necessary instructions to the cognizant contract administration office and acting on recommendations submitted by that office (see part 42 and 46.104(f));
L1:(d) When contract administration is retained (see part 42), verifying that the contractor fulfills the contract quality requirements; and
L1:(e) Ensuring that nonconformances are identified, and establishing the significance of a nonconformance when considering the acceptability of supplies or services which do not meet contract requirements.

46.104 Contract administration office responsibilities

L0:When a contract is assigned for administration to the contract administration office cognizant of the contractor’s plant, that office, unless specified otherwise, must—
L1:(a) Develop and apply efficient procedures for performing Government contract quality assurance actions under the contract in accordance with the written direction of the contracting office;
L1:(b) Perform all actions necessary to verify whether the supplies or services conform to contract quality requirements;
L1:(c) Maintain, as part of the performance records of the contract, suitable records reflecting-
L2:(1) The nature of Government contract quality assurance actions, including, when appropriate, the number of observations made and the number and type of defects; and
L2:(2) Decisions regarding the acceptability of the products, the processes, and the requirements, as well as action to correct defects.
L1:(d) Implement any specific written instructions from the contracting office;
L1:(e) Report to the contracting office any defects observed in design or technical requirements, including contract quality requirements; and
L1:(f) Recommend any changes necessary to the contract, specifications, instructions, or other requirements that will provide more effective operations or eliminate unnecessary costs (see 46.103(c)).

46.402 Government contract quality assurance at source

L0:Agencies must perform contract quality assurance, including inspection, at source if—
L1:(a) Performance at any other place would require uneconomical disassembly or destructive testing;
L1:(b) Considerable loss would result from the manufacture and shipment of unacceptable supplies, or from the delay in making necessary corrections;
L1:(c) Special required instruments, gauges, or facilities are available only at source;
L1:(d) Performance at any other place would destroy or require the replacement of costly special packing and packaging;
L1:(e) Government inspection during contract performance is essential; or
L1:(f) It is determined for other reasons to be in the Government’s interest.

46.403 Government contract quality assurance at destination

L1:(a) Government contract quality assurance that can be performed at destination is normally limited to inspection of the supplies or services. Inspection must be performed at destination under the following circumstances—
L2:(1) Supplies are purchased off-the-shelf and require no technical inspection;
L2:(2) Necessary testing equipment is located only at destination;
L2:(3) Perishable subsistence supplies purchased within the United States, except that those supplies destined for overseas shipment will normally be inspected for condition and quantity at points of embarkation;
L2:(4) Brand name products purchased for authorized resale through commissaries or similar facilities (however, supplies destined for direct overseas shipment may be accepted by the contracting officer or an authorized representative on the basis of a tally sheet evidencing receipt of shipment signed by the port transportation officer or other designated official at the transshipment point);
L2:(5) The products being purchased are processed under direct control of the National Institutes of Health or the Food and Drug Administration of the Department of Health and Human Services;
L2:(6) The contract is for services performed at destination; or
L2:(7) It is determined for other reasons to be in the Government’s interest.
L1:(b) Overseas inspection of supplies shipped from the United States must not be required except in unusual circumstances, and then only when the contracting officer determines in advance that inspection can be performed or makes necessary arrangements for its performance.

46.703 Criteria for use of warranties

L0:The use of warranties is not mandatory. In determining whether a warranty is appropriate for a specific acquisition, the contracting officer must consider the following factors:
L1:(a) Nature and use of the supplies or services. This includes such factors as-
L2:(1) Complexity and function;
L2:(2) Degree of development;
L2:(3) State of the art;
L2:(4) End use;
L2:(5) Difficulty in detecting defects before acceptance; and
L2:(6) Potential harm to the Government if the item is defective.
L1:(b) Cost. Warranty costs arise from-
L2:(1) The contractor’s charge for accepting the deferred liability created by the warranty; and
L2:(2) Government administration and enforcement of the warranty (see paragraph (c) of this section).
L1:(c) Administration and enforcement. The Government’s ability to enforce the warranty is essential to the effectiveness of any warranty. There must be some assurance that an adequate administrative system for reporting defects exists or can be established. The adequacy of a reporting system may depend upon such factors as the—
L2:(1) Nature and complexity of the item;
L2:(2) Location and proposed use of the item;
L2:(3) Storage time for the item;
L2:(4) Distance of the using activity from the source of the item;
L2:(5) Difficulty in establishing existence of defects; and
L2:(6) Difficulty in tracing responsibility for defects.
L1:(d) Trade practice. In many instances an item is customarily warranted in the trade, and, as a result of that practice, the cost of an item to the Government will be the same whether or not a warranty is included. In those instances, it would be in the Government’s interest to include such a warranty.
L1:(e) Reduced requirements. The contractor’s charge for assumption of added liability may be partially or completely offset by reducing the Government’s contract quality assurance requirements where the warranty provides adequate assurance of a satisfactory product.

47.304-5 Exceptions

L1:(a) Unusual conditions or circumstances might dictate the use of terms other than f.o.b. origin or f.o.b. destination. Such conditions or circumstances include, but are not limited to—
L2:(1) Transportation disabilities at origin or destination;
L2:(2) Mode of transportation required;
L2:(3) Availability of Government or commercial loading, unloading, or transshipment facilities;
L2:(4) Characteristics of the supplies;
L2:(5) Trade customs related to certain supplies;
L2:(6) Origins or destinations in Alaska and Hawaii; and
L2:(7) Program requirements.
L1:(b) Obtain assistance from transportation officers before issuing solicitations when unusual conditions or circumstances exist that relate to f.o.b. terms.

49.104 Duties of prime contractor after receipt of notice of termination

L0:After receiving a termination notice, the contractor is required to follow the notice and the termination clause of the contract, except as otherwise directed by the TCO. The notice and clause for convenience terminations generally require the contractor to—
L1:(a) Stop work immediately on the terminated portion of the contract and stop placing subcontracts for that work;
L1:(b) Terminate all subcontracts related to the terminated portion of the prime contract;
L1:(c) Immediately tell the TCO about any special circumstances that prevent stopping work;
L1:(d) Perform the continued portion of the contract and submit promptly any request for an equitable adjustment of price for the continued portion, if the termination is partial. Support this request with evidence of any cost increases;
L1:(e) Take necessary or directed action to protect and preserve property in the contractor's possession in which the Government has or may acquire an interest. When directed by the TCO, deliver this property to the Government;
L1:(f) Promptly notify the TCO in writing of any legal proceedings arising from any subcontract or other commitment related to the terminated portion of the contract;
L1:(g) Settle outstanding liabilities and proposals arising from termination of subcontracts. Get any approvals or ratifications the TCO requires;
L1:(h) Promptly submit the contractor's own settlement proposal, supported by appropriate schedules; and
L1:(i) Dispose of termination inventory as directed or authorized by the TCO.

49.109-2 Reservations

L1:(a) The TCO must —
L2:(1) Reserve in the settlement agreement any rights or demands of the parties that are excluded from the settlement;
L2:(2) Ensure reservation wording does not create rights for the parties beyond those existing before executing the settlement agreement;
L2:(3) Mark each applicable settlement agreement with "This settlement agreement contains a reservation" and keep in the contract file until the reservation is removed;
L2:(4) Ensure sufficient funds are kept to cover complete settlement of reserved items; and
L2:(5) At the appropriate time, prepare a separate settlement of reserved items and include it in a separate settlement agreement.
L1:(b) A recommended format for settlement of reservations appears in 49.603-9.

49.109-6 Joint settlement of two or more settlement proposals

L1:(a) With contractor consent, the TCO or TCOs concerned may jointly negotiate two or more termination settlement proposals with the same contractor under different contracts, even if the contracts are with different contracting offices or agencies. Consolidate accounting work as much as practical. The resulting settlement may be shown by—
L2:(1) One settlement agreement covering all contracts involved; or
L2:(2) A separate agreement for each contract involved.
L1:(b) When the settlement agreement covers more than one contract, it must—
L2:(1) Clearly identify the contracts involved;
L2:(2) Assign an amendment modification number to each contract;
L2:(3) Divide the total settlement amount among the contracts on some reasonable basis;
L2:(4) Have attached or incorporated a schedule showing the division; and
L2:(5) Be distributed and attached to each contract involved the same way as other contract modifications.

49.204 Deductions

L0:From the settlement payment, the TCO must subtract—
L1:(a) The price of any inventory kept or purchased by the contractor, plus proceeds from materials sold but not yet paid or credited to the Government;
L1:(b) The fair value, as determined by the TCO, of any inventory that was lost, damaged beyond usability, or not delivered to the Government (normal spoilage is expected, as is inventory for which the Government has expressly assumed the risk of loss);
L1:(c) Any other appropriate amounts for that specific case.

49.305-2 Construction contracts

L1:(a) The percentage of completion basis includes all contractor effort, not just the actual construction work. It includes such factors as—
L2:(1) Mobilization including organization;
L2:(2) Use of finances;
L2:(3) Ordering and receiving materials;
L2:(4) Placing subcontracts;
L2:(5) Creating shop drawings;
L2:(6) Performing work in place by own personnel;
L2:(7) Supervising subcontractors;
L2:(8) Managing the job; and
L2:(9) Closing down the project;
L1:(b) Calculate the fee adjustment as follows:
L2:(1) Assign a weighted value to each factor based on importance and difficulty.
L2:(2) The total weighted value should be easily divisible (e.g., by 100) to determine percentages.
L2:(3) Determine the percentage complete of each factor based on specific facts of each contract.
L2:(4) Multiply the completion percentage by the importance value for each factor.
L2:(5) Add these values for overall completion percentage.
L2:(6) Apply this percentage to the total fee applicable to the terminated portion of the contract to calculate the equitable adjustment.

